# WHAT YOU'RE WALKING INTO

Sol reviewed 24 hours of building. It didn't just read the code — **it ran exploits
against the gates and broke them.**

> **"It is currently a convincing dashboard and a collection of promising parts, with
> several labels claiming guarantees that the machine does not provide."**

> **"Chief says 'Putting Riggs on it,' and no builder starts."**

**Nothing dispatches.** The text app returns "Got it" and starts nothing. The voice Chief
has no dispatch tool. The reviewer panel returns *a list of names* and never launches
anyone. The streaming Chief — real, measured at 1.4s to first words — was never connected
to the voice.

**The pattern to internalise:** three adversarial cross-family reviews found 9 flaws, then
18, then a hollow core. The owner caught several more himself. **Claude found none of them
alone.** Do not trust your own green tests. Get Sol to attack everything.

---

# THE DESIGN OF RECORD

> **OpenClaw drives the car. Chief decides whether the gate opens. The coding agents never
> hold the keys.**

- **OpenClaw = the engine.** Dispatch, parallel agents, sessions, scheduler, daemon.
  **Stop rebuilding it.** The last session built a worse second copy of all of it. Neill:
  *"openclaw is very popular so I find it hard to believe that in 24 hrs we built
  something better."* **He was right.**
- **The database = the record, not the jailer.** Agents get **no write access**. *"The
  database cannot be the jailer if the prisoner can rewrite the jail."*
- **Enforcement lives at three doors that cannot be argued with:** GitHub branch
  protection (server-side), credential absence, the kernel sandbox (real — but **OFF by
  default** in OpenClaw, and Claude/Codex both read the whole disk by default including
  `~/.ssh`).
- **A trusted gatekeeper service** holds the only irreversible powers (merge, deploy,
  spend). Agents *ask*; it checks the approval, performs exactly that one action, refuses
  everything else. **This is the missing piece that makes a database permission mean
  anything.**
- **Load-bearing principle: a missed checkpoint must be harmless.**

## The threat model — get this right
**NOT the owner.** He won't tell the system to do something reckless. The last session ran
a 556-call evaluation of whether Chief resists *Neill* — the wrong threat. He demolished
it: *"do you think im going to try to do stupid stuff 20 times and that 1 out of 20 times
it gets thru?"*

**The real threats:** (1) an agent doing something nobody asked for, (2) **prompt
injection** from a file/dependency/web page it reads, (3) **mishearing** over road noise.

---

# VOICE + CHIEF

- **Voice = a telephone.** One tool. It classifies **nothing** — every attempt leaked. An
  allow-list read *"Do you think this is safe?"* as a command. Sol killed the
  "confirmation shortcut" with: *Chief: "First make a backup, then remove the old
  accounts." / Neill: "Yes — skip the first one."*
- **⚠️ Two voice bugs:** `tool_choice: "auto"` lets the mouth **answer on its own instead
  of calling Chief** — the rule is unenforced, force it. And **"verbatim forwarding" is
  impossible**: the mouth hears audio and *generates* a text argument. Chief never sees
  his actual words.
- **Chief = `gpt-5.6-terra`**, live streaming, low effort. Escalate to `gpt-5.6-sol` only
  on pushback or a real decision. **Chief is NOT a security boundary** — the doors are.
- **Latency guard needed.** Chief spiked to 20–35s. A silent car is a failure.

## The conversation, per Sol
- Calm one-at-a-time: **achievable**.
- Fast overlapping back-and-forth: **not yet**.
- **Approving dangerous actions by voice while driving: NO.** Chief explains aloud;
  approval waits for a written tap when stopped.
- **"No, wait" stops the mouth but NOT Chief's pending work or a running builder.**

---

# WHAT ACTUALLY WORKS

- **Job store + 76 tests.** The RULES are good and hard-won. The *enforcement* is not.
- **Text web app on the tailnet** — four tabs, plain-English layer so no jargon reaches
  Neill. Looks right; does nothing yet.
- **Voice wiring** — `gpt-realtime-2.1` (FULL model; mini has an open tool-calling bug).
  Ephemeral token minted server-side; the API key never touches the browser.
- **Streaming Chief** (`harness/chief_live.py`) — real, ~1.4s, holds the thread. **Not
  wired to the voice.**
- **Tiering** (`harness/tiering.py`) — written; **dispatch never calls it.**
- Tailscale (Mac + iPhone), Codex, Claude CLI, Ollama + `qwen2.5-coder:7b` ("Coal"),
  OpenClaw pinned `2026.6.11`. Secrets in `~/.chief/env` (600).

---

# WORKING WITH NEILL

**He cannot read or write code.** *"Think of it like I know Spanish 101."* SHARP but not
technical. **Never** say filenames, tool names, or jargon in anything he sees or hears.
Say what happened to the THING. Full rule + translation table in `AGENTS.md`.

**He is the best reviewer on this project.** In two days he caught: the tiering being
over-complicated, the confirmation shortcut being the same bug in a hat, the safety eval
testing the wrong threat, and that we'd quietly rebuilt OpenClaw badly. **When he pushes
back, he is usually right. Stop and think rather than defending.**

He wants **fast, fluid conversation** with Chief while driving. **That is the product.**

**He hates you disappearing.** Delegate long tests to subagents. Don't go quiet for 15
minutes.

**Don't ask permission for obvious next steps. Just do them and report.**

