docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:71:  on, strict, with a narrow domain allow-list (§3).
docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:76:- **(a) Wasted work and wasted spend inside the sandbox** — the agent burns tokens/rate
docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:77:  limit doing something pointless in its worktree. Bounded by the budget guard and the
docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:78:  emergency stop (§7). Not dangerous, just wasteful.
docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:82:  release requires evidence the harness captured, not text the agent wrote.
docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:84:  instructions *within its own sandbox*. It can follow the injection right up to the
docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:86:  code into its worktree — which is exactly what the review gauntlet and branch
docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:103:- GitHub branch protection — GitHub's servers.
docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:105:- The kernel sandbox — Seatbelt, kernel-enforced.
docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:106:- Filesystem ownership of the safety database — the agent user physically cannot open it
docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:108:- The gatekeeper holding the only copies of dangerous credentials — an agent that asks
docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:116:  safety boundary.** They are integrity checks on the ledger the gatekeeper reads.
docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:130:it go through the hook?" but "if it *doesn't*, what stops it?" — which is always one of
docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:131:the real boundaries in §2, never the hook alone.
docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:133:| Seam | Can it skip the pre-action hook? | What stops harm anyway |
docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:135:| **Different agent runners** (Claude Code, Codex, Ollama) | Yes — each has its own config; one will. | Branch protection, credential absence, sandbox, no DB write, disposable worktree. The runner that skips still holds no keys. |
docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:136:| **MCP servers** an agent connects to | Yes — an MCP tool acts directly, the hook may never see it. | The MCP server runs inside the agent's sandbox and under the agent user; it inherits the same absent credentials and network allow-list. An MCP server that needs a real credential can't get one. New MCP servers are allow-listed, not auto-trusted. |
docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:137:| **Agent hooks / tool wrappers** the runner itself fires | Yes | Same as runners — they execute as the sandboxed agent user. A hook can't grant itself a door. |
docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:138:| **Scheduled / overnight jobs** (OpenClaw scheduler) | They run *unattended*, so a skipped check has no human nearby. | Overnight jobs get the *most* restricted profile: local/subscription seats only, no dangerous capability can be requested while unattended (approvals require a live read-back, which needs the owner awake — see §6). Worst case overnight is wasted tokens and code sitting in a branch for morning review. |
docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:139:| **The voice path** | The voice holds one tool and no credentials; it can't act. | It reaches Chief, Chief reaches the harness, the harness dispatches. Nothing on the voice path can perform a dangerous action; it can only *ask*. |
docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:140:| **The gatekeeper / release service itself** | It is the one thing that *must* act. | It is the smallest, most-reviewed component, runs as a separate trusted user, authenticates its callers, and re-verifies every request against records only it can write (§4). It is the single trusted computing base — kept tiny on purpose. |
docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:141:| **OpenClaw upgrades** | A new version can change defaults (sandbox off, exec policy loosened) silently. | Version-pinned to `2026.6.11`; upgrades are deliberate. Every upgrade re-runs the full attack test (§8) before it's trusted, and the sandbox/exec-policy settings are asserted in a startup check that refuses to run if they've drifted. |
docs/ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md:144:all at the hook. We close them at the doors, so a leaky seam is harmless.**
