# Chief Command — Open TODOs

**Persistent across sessions. Read at the start of every session. Update as things are
done — don't let this rot.**

Last updated: 2026-07-14 (session 2 — commits landed, exploits verified, arch rewritten, Sol re-review running)

---

## 🔴 NEILL'S TASKS — only he can do these

These are the highest-leverage items on the entire project. Ask him about them at the
start of a session if they're still open.

- [ ] **Rotate the xAI API key.** It was pasted into a chat transcript. Real money.
      → `console.x.ai`
- [ ] **Rotate the OpenAI platform key.** Same — pasted into a transcript. Real money.
      → `platform.openai.com` → API keys
      *(After rotating, update `~/.chief/env` — it's chmod 600, not in the repo.)*
- [ ] **Turn on GitHub branch protection for `main`.** ~5 minutes.
      **This is the single highest-leverage safety item in the project.** It permanently
      blocks force-push and direct writes to main, enforced on GitHub's servers — where
      no agent and no model can argue past it. It works whether or not any of our code
      does.
- [ ] **Get production credentials off this machine** — shell profiles, `.env` files, the
      environment that launches agents. If an agent can't authenticate, no gate is
      needed. **This is the only real defence against prompt-injection exfiltration**,
      because the agent is *authorized* to read the repo.
- [ ] **Decide: does Grok touch the Arch EMR repo?** Recommendation: **NO**, pending
      independent verification. A wire-level analysis alleges Grok's CLI uploads entire
      repos — including `.env` secrets — to xAI. Single-source and unverified, but there
      is PHI in that repo, and no sandbox helps (the agent is allowed to read it).

---

## 🟠 SECURITY — ours to do

- [ ] **Regenerate the OpenClaw gateway token.** Sol found it in a readable backup
      settings file. *Context: Claude generated this token during setup; it is a LOCAL
      token for a service bound to Neill's tailnet, not a cloud credential. Lower stakes
      than the API keys — regenerate, don't panic.*
- [ ] **Turn the kernel sandbox ON, strict.** It's real (Seatbelt, kernel-enforced) but:
      - OpenClaw's sandbox is **OFF by default**
      - Claude Code and Codex both **read the entire disk by default**, including
        `~/.ssh` and `~/.aws`
      - Needs: `allowUnsandboxedCommands: false`, deny credential paths, scrub credential
        env vars, narrow the allowed domains (**never** a bare `github.com` wildcard —
        domain fronting)
- [ ] **Never set OpenClaw's exec policy to `full`/YOLO** — its own docs say that launches
      Claude Code with `--permission-mode bypassPermissions`. Use `auto` or `allowlist`.
- [ ] **Real auth on the phone app.** Being on the tailnet is not proof of being Neill.

---

## 🔵 BEFORE ANY BUILDING — design freeze is in effect

Neill's instruction: *"stop building new shit until we have this all nailed down, and
sol has reviewed it and approved it as well."*

- [x] **Verify Sol's exploits against the schema.** DONE 2026-07-14. All three
      reproduced against the real schema. Two fixed in `schema.sql` (verdicts now
      un-deletable; approvals can't be born granted) with regression tests in
      `test_sol_attacks.py` — 79 tests pass. The third (nonexistent screenshot path) is
      unfixable by a trigger and became a gatekeeper requirement in the arch doc.
      Transcripts now in `docs/sol/` (survive reboot).
- [x] **Rewrite `ARCHITECTURE_v2_2026-07-14.md`** DONE 2026-07-14. Restructured around
      Sol's 8 review questions; added the seam table, the plain-file-DB trust answer, the
      credential-absence-via-separate-user answer, and folded in the verified exploits.
- [~] **Send it back to Sol** (`./ask-sol.sh`). IN PROGRESS — round 4 review running.
      Iterate attack → fix → re-attack **until Sol has nothing left.**
- [ ] **Only then build.**

---

## 🟡 THE BUILD, once approved

### Make it actually work (it currently doesn't)
- [ ] **Dispatch.** Nothing runs today. *"Chief says 'Putting Riggs on it,' and no
      builder starts."* The text app returns "Got it" and starts nothing; the voice Chief
      has no dispatch tool.
- [ ] **Wire the streaming Chief to the voice.** `chief_live.py` is real and hits ~1.4s
      to first words — and is **not connected**. The voice path still spawns a fresh
      process per utterance, which is the exact lag it was built to fix.
- [ ] **Make the gauntlet actually run.** `run_gauntlet()` returns a *list of names* and
      never launches anyone.
- [ ] **One worktree per agent.** They currently all share one real project folder.
- [ ] **Use the tiering.** It's written; dispatch never calls it.

### The two voice bugs Sol found
- [ ] **Force the tool call.** `tool_choice: "auto"` lets the mouth **answer on its own
      instead of calling Chief** — the whole "everything goes to Chief" rule is
      unenforced.
- [ ] **Accept that "verbatim forwarding" is impossible.** The mouth hears audio and
      *generates* a text argument — that's another model interpreting him, not a
      transcript. Design around it.

### Fix the record layer
- [ ] **Tie reviews to a version.** Today: approve version A, builder changes it to B,
      **the old approval still counts.** Sol calls this the most dangerous flaw — it
      produces *"believable green checks on code nobody reviewed."*
- [ ] **A fail must condemn a VERSION, not the job forever.** Right now a fixed build can
      never ship.
- [ ] **Block DELETING verdicts** (editing fail→pass is blocked; deleting isn't).
- [ ] **Reserve budget BEFORE the provider call.** Refusing to *record* a charge doesn't
      unspend the money.
- [ ] **Agents get NO write access to the safety database.** *"The database cannot be the
      jailer if the prisoner can rewrite the jail."*

### The missing pieces
- [ ] **A trusted gatekeeper service** holding all irreversible powers (merge, deploy,
      spend). Agents *ask*; it checks the approval, does exactly that one thing, refuses
      everything else. **This is what makes a database permission mean anything.**
- [ ] **A hard emergency stop** that doesn't depend on Chief being alive.
- [ ] **Separate cancellation** for: the speech, Chief's current thought, queued work, a
      running builder. *("No, wait" currently stops the mouth but NOT the work.)*
- [ ] **Numbered conversation turns** so an old answer can't arrive after a newer
      correction.
- [ ] **Duplicate protection** so a retry can't start the same job twice.
- [ ] **Crash recovery** — reconcile jobs after sleep, power loss, network failure,
      OpenClaw restart.
- [ ] **Latency guard.** Chief spiked to 20–35s. A silent car is a failure — hard timeout
      → *"I'm struggling, nothing's started."*
- [ ] **A realistic driving test set** — road noise, passengers, radio, weak signal,
      interruptions, self-corrections.

---

## ⚫ RULES THAT ARE SETTLED — don't relitigate

- **Voice = telephone.** It classifies nothing. Every attempt leaked.
- **Chief is NOT a security boundary.** The doors are (GitHub, missing credentials, the
  kernel).
- **The threat is NOT Neill.** It's (1) agents doing unasked things, (2) prompt
  injection, (3) mishearing. Don't build ceremony that guards against him.
- **No approving dangerous actions by voice while driving.** Chief explains aloud;
  approval waits for a written tap when stopped.
- **Adopt OpenClaw as the engine. Stop rebuilding it.**
- **Get Sol to attack everything. Don't trust green tests.** Three reviews found 9 flaws,
  then 18, then a hollow core. Claude found none of them itself.

codex
The core claim does not survive the full walk-through. Even with the stated controls, an agent can plant harmful code that later passes fallible model reviews and is deliberately carried through the release door. It can also leak private project content through the model connection it must be allowed to use, exhaust the Mac’s resources, consume subscription limits outside the local budget record, and damage shared Git storage. Those are not merely “wasted work and trust.”
Completed the document and live-harness review. The design still has load-bearing gaps in isolation, release trust, data egress, evidence provenance, and voice state control, so the approval verdict is no.
  ✓ Read the corrected architecture and its referenced safety claims in full
  ✓ Inspect the live harness where the document relies on current behavior
  ✓ Trace bypasses, release authority, credential exposure, and voice failure cases
  ✓ Deliver an approval verdict with required changes ranked by importance
codex
No. I do not approve this design for construction.

The central failure is this:

> An agent can put a harmful payload into its working copy, fool the model reviewers, receive genuine passing records, and have the release service deliberately deploy it. Once deployed, that code gains access to production data and credentials.

No database forgery or direct release-service call is required. Every stated door can work exactly as designed and the harmful action still occurs.

## 1. “A missed checkpoint must be harmless”

The design does not achieve this.

Assume one agent runner ignores the pre-action check.

What the controls genuinely stop:

- It cannot directly overwrite the protected main branch, if GitHub is configured correctly and the agent has no administrative account.
- It cannot directly open the safety database for writing, if it really runs under a different account.
- It cannot directly call production providers without credentials.
- It cannot read the owner’s files outside its sandbox, if the sandbox is correct and actually applied to the runner and every child process.

But serious harm remains.

### Harmful code can travel through the approved route

The agent adds a hidden data-stealing routine, destructive database command, malicious package, or altered deployment instruction to its branch.

The reviewers inspect it. The code contains instructions aimed at misleading those reviewers, or the defect is simply subtle. They pass it. The tester exercises the happy path and captures a real screenshot. The database honestly records genuine passes for the exact version. The release service merges and deploys it.

The deployed program now has the production authority the agent itself lacked. It can leak customer information, delete data, run up cloud charges, or weaken security.

That is the biggest remaining hole. Branch protection controls who may merge. It does not prove that merged code is safe. A screenshot proves one visible flow worked; it does not prove there is no hidden payload.

### Private information can still leave the machine

The document considers credentials but ignores everything else worth protecting:

- Private source code
- Business plans
- Issue contents
- Conversation history
- Project memory
- Customer information
- Medical information in other projects

The agent must be allowed to communicate with its model provider. A poisoned instruction can make it include private material in a model request. A narrow internet list does not help when the permitted destination is itself a channel capable of carrying the data out.

### A GitHub token can cause damage outside the main branch

If the agent can open its own pull request, it probably has some form of GitHub writing authority. Unless that authority is extremely narrow, it can potentially:

- Overwrite or delete unprotected branches
- Create misleading pull requests and comments
- Create tags or releases
- Trigger paid automation repeatedly
- Cause untrusted branch code to run in automation that has secrets
- Disrupt other agents’ work

Protecting only the main branch is insufficient.

### The “disposable working copy” is not isolated

Git working copies normally share the underlying project history, branches, settings, and stored objects. The live repository already has many such shared working copies.

An agent that must be allowed to create commits can potentially corrupt shared history, change shared branch records, damage other agents’ branches, or interfere with the real local project. Deleting its visible folder does not undo that.

A genuinely disposable agent area needs a separate clone or separate storage image, not a standard shared working copy.

### It can damage the owner’s Mac by exhaustion

The proposed sandbox does not specify limits on:

- Memory
- Processor use
- Disk consumption
- Number of child processes
- Network traffic
- Length of execution

An agent can fill the disk, consume all memory, create thousands of processes, or make the Mac unusable. A provider budget does not stop any of those.

### It can consume model limits outside the budget record

A coding runner needs some authentication to use its subscription or provider. If it can call the runner directly, it can consume subscription limits or paid calls without first reserving money in Chief Command.

The release service cannot hold “all spend authority” while agent runners and the voice system can themselves make provider calls.

The defensible core claim would be:

> A skipped checkpoint cannot directly use release credentials.

That is substantially weaker than “a skipped checkpoint is harmless.”

## 2. Guarantees that remain ineffective

These statements are still stronger than their enforcement.

### “The release service is the only trusted component”

False. The document gives the larger harness the trusted operating-system identity and allows it to write verdicts, approvals, job status, and evidence records.

Therefore the trusted base includes:

- The entire harness
- Its web interface
- Its model-response handling
- The dispatch channel
- The evidence-capture system
- OpenClaw integration
- The release service
- Deployment scripts
- The local operating-system account

The release service may be only a few hundred lines, but it trusts facts created by a much larger and more exposed system.

### “The database proves a reviewer passed the work”

It proves only that the trusted harness wrote a row saying so.

The design does not specify a protected identity for each reviewer response, how responses are bound to one run, or how a forged or replayed completion is rejected. More importantly, a genuine reviewer can be fooled. “The harness received this response” is not the same as “the reviewed code is safe.”

### “Harness-captured evidence is ground truth”

The existence of a screenshot is not ground truth about the whole release.

A real screenshot can still be:

- From the wrong build
- From a stale process
- From a harmless screen while hidden malicious behavior exists elsewhere
- Captured before a later change
- Produced by a test that never exercises the dangerous behavior

Evidence needs a protected connection to the exact built package, exact test environment, exact test instructions, and exact release candidate. Merely checking that a file exists is inadequate.

### “Spend is bounded”

Only the OpenAI provider’s own hard cap is a real financial boundary. The local spending record cannot stop calls that bypass it. Other providers and subscription limits are not covered by the stated $100 ceiling.

### “No dangerous approval while moving”

A written tap proves that somebody tapped. It does not prove Neill is stopped, understood the text, or was the person holding the phone.

This can be a safety instruction to Neill. It cannot be claimed as an enforced guarantee without stronger confirmation, such as a fresh biometric check and an interface that never represents “stopped” as a fact it cannot know.

### “Cancellation stops a running builder”

Sending a stop signal is a request, not a guarantee. The builder may already have acted, may ignore the signal, may have created child processes, or may be running remotely.

Containment can make the remaining work harmless. Cancellation itself cannot.

### “MCP servers and hooks inherit the sandbox”

That is an assumption. Some are local child processes; others are remote services, shared host services, or processes started before the sandbox applies. A remote service can hold its own credentials even when the agent does not.

### “Startup checks prevent configuration drift”

A startup check is still configuration. It helps detect mistakes, but it is not independent enforcement. The same compromised account or upgrade that changes the dangerous setting may also change or disable the check.

## 3. The seams where checkpoints can be skipped

| Path | Exact failure |
|---|---|
| Different agent runners | Each runner has its own commands, permission modes, extensions, child processes, login behavior, and direct network paths. A wrapper around one runner does not govern another. |
| MCP servers | A local server may start outside the sandbox. A remote server may hold credentials. A server can make follow-on calls or receive callbacks the harness never sees. |
| Hooks | Runner hooks, project hooks, package installation scripts, test setup, Git hooks, and automation scripts can execute under different permissions from the agent that caused them. |
| Scheduled work | OpenClaw scheduling, Mac startup jobs, retries, crash recovery, resumed sessions, and manually launched overnight jobs may use different accounts or sandbox profiles. An old approval could also remain live when unattended work resumes. |
| Voice | The voice model generates the words passed to Chief. The phone can replay a request. A partial utterance can be committed early. The browser can potentially call the voice service directly. A passenger or radio can speak into an already authenticated session. |
| Release service | Merge commands, deployment scripts, package installation, database changes, rollback routines, automation callbacks, and production health checks are all actions inside or after the release boundary. They need their own restrictions. |
| OpenClaw upgrades | Pinning OpenClaw does not pin runner programs, MCP servers, plugins, model behavior, provider APIs, operating-system behavior, packages, or deployment tools. An upgrade may also run data conversion under the trusted account before the startup check runs. |

There are additional seams at browser authentication, phone reconnection, GitHub automation, model-provider failover, evidence capture, and manual agent sessions started by the owner outside Chief Command.

## 4. The release service

This is not adequately designed.

### What can compromise it

- A flaw in its request parser or authentication
- A flaw in the larger trusted harness
- A stolen phone or browser session
- A malicious dependency in the release service
- A malicious build or installation script run during deployment
- A compromised operating-system account
- A leaked credential in logs, backups, process settings, or configuration
- An overly powerful GitHub or production credential
- A malicious package that runs while the release service’s credentials are available
- A race where the checked version changes before the merge or deployment completes

The most dangerous case is the release service executing untrusted project code while holding release credentials. It must never build, install, test, or run agent-produced code in the same environment that contains those credentials.

### Why the plain database is not sufficient

File permissions can prevent the agent account from editing the database. That part is real.

But the document lets the trusted harness run as the release account and write the database. Compromising the harness therefore gives an attacker both the record and the release caller’s identity.

The release service must be the only writer of release facts. The larger harness should submit signed or strongly authenticated observations through a narrow interface. It should not share the release account.

Even then, the database proves only that observations were recorded. Each review and artifact must be tied to:

- One exact request
- One exact source version
- One exact base version
- One exact built package
- One exact reviewer run
- One exact test run
- One exact proposed release action

### What must prevent direct calls

“Different user” is not enough as a design description. It needs a concrete mechanism:

- No general network address agents can reach
- A local operating-system channel that checks the caller’s actual account
- No reusable password or token stored in an agent-readable file
- No arbitrary command parameter
- A fixed list of narrowly defined actions
- Exact repository, version, environment, and action parameters
- Single-use request numbers
- Atomic consumption of approvals before acting
- Rejection if main, the artifact, or the approval changes during the operation

The trusted harness also must not become a messenger that blindly makes release requests on an agent’s behalf. Release requests must come from a deterministic controller that derives them from protected state, not from model-written text.

## 5. Credential absence on the owner’s Mac

“Absent from the agent’s reach” is possible in principle. The proposed first-operation baseline is too fragile for this particular machine.

Credentials leak back through:

- Subscription login tokens needed by the coding runners
- Git credential helpers
- Login shells and startup settings
- SSH agent connections
- Browser cookies and saved sessions
- OpenClaw settings and backup settings
- MCP server configuration
- Cloud command-line settings
- Repository environment files
- Shell history
- Chat transcripts
- Logs and crash reports
- Shared temporary folders
- Clipboard contents
- Container-management connections
- Locally listening services that act with the owner’s authority

There is also the ordinary operational failure: Neill or an agent launches a coding runner manually under Neill’s everyday account. That run has the owner’s authority regardless of how safely the managed launcher is configured.

The runner authentication problem is unresolved. If Codex or Claude runs inside the agent area using subscription login, some usable authentication material must be available to it. That material should be held by a separate broker, not placed inside the agent account.

For this personal Mac, a dedicated virtual machine with no owner-home sharing, no clipboard sharing, no credential connections, and no host-management connection should be the minimum. The release service and production credentials should be outside that virtual machine. A separate physical machine would be stronger.

“Put the release service elsewhere later” is not acceptable if credential absence is a load-bearing first-operation guarantee.

## 6. Voice and Chief

There are several unresolved failures.

### The voice is not a pure relay

It interprets audio into tool arguments and then speaks Chief’s answer “in its own words.” It can change meaning in both directions.

Warnings, numbers, consequences, confirmations, and failure reports must be delivered exactly, without voice-model paraphrasing.

### A single tool conflicts with the independent emergency stop

If the voice has only one tool and that tool calls Chief, then “stop everything” still depends on Chief being alive and responsive.

A real emergency stop requires a separate, hardwired path that does not use Chief or model judgment. While driving, that path also must be reachable without navigating a screen.

### Numbered turns do not undo an action

A turn number can suppress an old answer. It cannot undo a dispatch that already occurred.

“Delete the—no, archive it” remains dangerous if the partial turn is committed and dispatched before the correction arrives. The controller needs explicit states such as:

- Still listening
- Complete but not committed
- Committed
- Superseded
- Dispatched
- Cancel requested
- Confirmed stopped

Only committed instructions may dispatch. A model’s guess that speech is complete is not sufficient for dangerous or costly work.

### Passengers and radio speech remain authorized

Phone authentication proves which phone opened the session. It does not prove who is speaking into an open microphone.

A passenger, radio advertisement, podcast, or navigation voice can issue a routine dispatch or stop instruction. Voice recognition is not strong enough to become an approval boundary, but the design needs an explicit response to ambient speakers.

### The eight-second message may be false

Chief can call a tool before beginning its spoken answer. A network delay can then trigger “nothing’s started” after work has actually started.

The system may say “nothing started” only after it has cancelled the turn and confirmed from the authoritative job record that no dispatch exists. Otherwise it must say it lost confirmation and direct Neill to the emergency stop or written status.

### “Stopped and tapped” is not enforceable

The system cannot know that the car is stopped merely because the screen was tapped. Do not state this as a machine guarantee.

### Long conversation history is unsafe as operational memory

Summaries can lose a “not,” revive a cancelled instruction, mix projects, retain prompt injection, or misstate an approval.

Conversation summaries may help natural dialogue. They must never determine:

- Current job state
- Which instruction is active
- Whether something was cancelled
- What version was approved
- Whether an approval is live
- Which project is in scope

Those facts need structured records outside Chief’s conversation.

The design also omits long-session reconnection, session rollover, lost audio, phone calls, navigation interruptions, switching networks, and restoration of the exact conversation state after a voice session expires.

Terra being Chief is not itself the problem. But Chief still decides routing, task wording, and whether a correction supersedes an earlier instruction. Those decisions require deterministic controls around the model.

## 7. What is missing entirely

The document still lacks:

1. A precise map of every process, operating-system account, connection, storage area, credential, and allowed network destination.

2. A real release protocol covering caller identity, exact allowed actions, single-use requests, races, failure recovery, and credential scope.

3. A clean build-and-release chain. The exact reviewed source must produce an identified package, and that exact package—not a rebuilt approximation—must be deployed.

4. Protection against malicious code that passes model review. High-risk changes such as dependencies, deployment instructions, authentication, billing, database changes, and the release service itself need a qualified technical human review or must be prohibited from automatic release.

5. Protection of private information other than credentials. There is no rule controlling what source code, customer data, memory, or transcripts may be sent to each model provider.

6. Exact GitHub protections for branches, tags, automation, packages, releases, administrative bypass, and production environments.

7. Resource limits for processor use, memory, disk, child processes, execution time, and network volume.

8. Tested backups and restoration for the safety record, job history, project memory, configuration, and approval history. This was identified in the previous review and disappeared from the corrected architecture.

9. Tamper-resistant audit records stored somewhere other than the same Mac and same trusted account.

10. Incident response: emergency credential revocation, device loss, compromised phone sessions, poisoned project memory, and compromised release-service recovery.

11. Protection of the reviewers themselves from instructions hidden in code, test output, issues, web pages, and dependencies.

12. Project sensitivity rules. Medical information and ordinary source code cannot share the same provider and network policy merely because both are “inside a sandbox.”

13. A complete list of pinned components. Pinning OpenClaw alone leaves runners, plugins, MCP servers, packages, model aliases, and deployment tooling able to change.

14. Proof that the emergency stop terminates remote runs and all child processes, not merely the visible local process.

## 8. Required changes before approval

Ranked by importance:

1. **Move all agent execution into a dedicated virtual machine or separate host.** No owner files, credentials, browser data, clipboard sharing, host-management connection, or shared project history. Put the release service outside that environment.

2. **Make the release service the sole owner and writer of release state.** The general harness must run under a different identity and must not be able to write verdicts, approvals, evidence provenance, or released status directly.

3. **Treat agent-produced code as an untrusted payload even after model review.** Require qualified technical human review for changes that can gain production authority, alter dependencies, change automation, handle secrets, change authentication or money, modify stored data, or change the release system. Otherwise prohibit automatic release of those changes.

4. **Replace shared working copies with isolated clones or storage snapshots.** Agents must not share Git history, branch records, hooks, or project settings.

5. **Remove direct GitHub writing authority from agents.** Agents submit a patch or package to a broker. The broker creates the branch and pull request. Protect branches, tags, automation, packages, releases, production environments, and administrative settings.

6. **Define an exact package-based release chain.** Review and test a clean package built from the proposed version combined with the current main branch. Bind every verdict and artifact to its digest. Deploy that same package. Never run untrusted build or installation code beside release credentials.

7. **Broker all model and provider access.** Agent runners receive no reusable subscription or API credentials. The broker applies per-job limits before every call. Hard provider-side caps must exist for every metered provider.

8. **Add an enforced data-egress policy.** Identify what each project may send to each provider. Block customer, medical, private, and cross-project information independently of credential scanning.

9. **Specify and test the full containment profile.** Include every runner, child process, MCP server, hook, scheduled job, retry, and recovery path. Add processor, memory, disk, process, time, and network limits.

10. **Replace the voice flow with a deterministic turn controller.** No dispatch from partial speech; authoritative acknowledgements only; exact warnings without paraphrasing; a separate emergency-stop path; structured state outside conversation history; reconnection and long-session tests.

11. **Add off-machine backups, tamper-resistant audit records, restoration drills, credential-revocation procedures, and device-loss handling.**

12. **Rewrite the core claim.** The design cannot honestly promise that a missed checkpoint is harmless. It can promise that a missed checkpoint cannot directly obtain release authority, while production containment, independent review, and narrow deployment privileges limit the harm of code that passes review.

The current live system also confirms that these are not academic distinctions: the safety database is still owned by the ordinary account, reviews are not tied to an exact version, the reviewer panel does not launch reviewers, and the voice tool remains optional. The document admits most of that is unbuilt, so those facts are not the reason for rejection. The rejection is because the proposed end state would still allow harmful code to pass honestly through every gate.
